WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:06.229
Netflix's latest original feature, starring
Thor himself, Chris Hemsworth, alongside Bollywood

00:00:06.229 --> 00:00:11.900
sensation Randeep Hooda, is about as high-octane
a thriller as any action fan could hope for.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:16.700
Through all the captivating action sequences
and frenetic fight scenes, Extraction actually

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:21.810
manages to tell a pretty complex story with
an ambiguous ending that has fans abuzz with

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.030
theories.

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:26.920
Extraction follows protagonist Tyler Rake,
a former member of the Australian Special

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:30.140
Air Service Regiment now working as an off-the-books
mercenary.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:35.320
At the film's outset, he's engaged by fellow
killer-for-hire Nik Khan to rescue the son

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:40.470
of India's leading drug kingpin, who is currently
being held for an exorbitant ransom by rival

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.710
Bangladeshi drug lord Amir Asif.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:48.160
A hiccup during Rake's attempted "extraction"
of his target brings down the wrath of Saju,

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:52.450
another former special operative currently
in the employ of the Bangladeshi drug lord.

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:55.730
Rake and Saju engage in a complex game of
cat and mouse…

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:56.730
"Ovi, it's Saju!

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:58.560
I'm here to take you home!"

00:00:58.560 --> 00:00:59.560
"Saju!

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:00.560
I'm here!"

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:01.560
"Sit down and shut up!"

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:05.750
But they ultimately end up working together
to outwit the authorities on their tails.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:10.150
As far as action-thriller set-ups go, it's
about as compelling as they come, but viewers

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:15.100
hoping for a clear-cut resolution that sees
charismatic anti-hero Rake victoriously walking

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:19.080
away from an explosion in slow motion were
left with a real head scratcher.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:23.200
Let's break Extraction's ambiguous ending
down with a little help from the film's director,

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:24.200
Sam Hargrave.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:28.909
At the beginning of the film, shark-eyed mercenary
Tyler Rake has an obvious death wish.

00:01:28.909 --> 00:01:33.810
His ambivalence toward his own continued survival
is a big part of what makes him such a deadly

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:34.810
merc.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:38.460
We learn that much of Rake's nihilism stems
from the death of his son, a loss that still

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:39.460
haunts him.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:43.850
It's his relationship with Ovi, his target
for extraction, that ultimately saves Rake

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:44.890
from himself.

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:49.600
As Rake begins to form a bond with Ovi, he
no longer sees the drug heir as a simple job,

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:53.130
but rather a kid whose life has become more
important than his own.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:55.680
"Can I ask you something?"

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:56.700
"It's Rake."

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:57.720
"What's Rake?"

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.250
"My last name."

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:04.479
By the time the film reaches its climactic
shootout on the bridge, Rake takes a mortal

00:02:04.479 --> 00:02:06.720
wound to ensure Ovi's safe escape.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:10.899
This emotional beat lands because we actually
believe that Rake no longer wants to die,

00:02:10.899 --> 00:02:13.290
but has chosen to give up his life to save
Ovi's.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:17.559
So Rake gets to go to his grave feeling like
he redeemed his soul, while Ovi lives to fight

00:02:17.559 --> 00:02:18.909
another day.

00:02:18.909 --> 00:02:20.310
Satisfying ending, right?

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:21.310
Not so fast.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:25.419
After Ovi's daring escape, the film presents
a very unexpected coda.

00:02:25.419 --> 00:02:30.159
In the final moments of Extraction, Ovi jumps
into a peaceful swimming pool, a mirror image

00:02:30.159 --> 00:02:32.469
of Rake's first scene jumping off a cliff.

00:02:32.469 --> 00:02:36.499
When he comes up for air, he sees a blurry
figure standing in the background who bears

00:02:36.499 --> 00:02:41.220
a striking resemblance to Rake, but the film
ends before we get any actual confirmation.

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:42.389
So, what gives?

00:02:42.389 --> 00:02:47.400
As it turns out, Extraction's final moments
were left open to interpretation by design.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.469
Hargrave weighed in on the mystery surrounding
this final scene.

00:02:50.469 --> 00:02:51.469
He told Collider:

00:02:51.469 --> 00:02:55.769
"We had a version of the movie, and we tested
it a lot, and it was not surprising that a

00:02:55.769 --> 00:03:00.079
lot of people wanted the character to live,
and some people wanted him to die...If people

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:04.680
on one hand feel like the story is complete
and is a story of redemption through sacrifice,

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:09.099
then for them, it'll be where the kid is imagining
[Rake standing there], and then now you go,

00:03:09.099 --> 00:03:10.329
'Yes, I'm satisfied.'"

00:03:10.329 --> 00:03:14.549
Hargrave went on to explain, quote, "If you
feel like you love Tyler Rake, and you love

00:03:14.549 --> 00:03:18.969
Chris Hemsworth, and you want a sequel, and
you're like 'There's no way, you can't kill

00:03:18.969 --> 00:03:21.799
him!' then that's Tyler Rake standing there
looking at you.

00:03:21.799 --> 00:03:25.769
So we kind of purposefully did not pull focus
to the character standing there."

00:03:25.769 --> 00:03:29.670
For fans looking for a little more closure,
Hargrave shared that the original screenplay

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:33.029
for Extraction included a much more definitive
ending.

00:03:33.029 --> 00:03:38.540
In screenwriter Joe Russo's intended story,
Tyler Rake very explicitly dies on the bridge,

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:42.249
completing the character arc he started when
he dove off that cliff in the initial scene.

00:03:42.249 --> 00:03:46.650
Hargrave told Collider that the test audiences
weren't crazy about the sacrifice, however.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:47.650
He dished:

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:49.870
"People were torn; it was almost down the
middle.

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:54.459
We want to appeal to as many people as possible
without compromising the integrity of the

00:03:54.459 --> 00:03:55.459
story.

00:03:55.459 --> 00:03:58.370
And so, we think a pretty good compromise
is to make an ambiguous ending."

00:03:58.370 --> 00:04:02.549
Hargrave ultimately admitted that he prefers
to imagine that Rake is dead by the film's

00:04:02.549 --> 00:04:06.329
conclusion, claiming that he felt as though
Rake had redeemed himself through sacrifice.

00:04:06.329 --> 00:04:11.029
For fans in desperate need of finality, that's
probably the best you're going to get.

00:04:11.029 --> 00:04:14.730
The rest of us will have to learn to live
with the ambiguity, at least until we get

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:16.100
an Extraction 2.

